reference:
Yes, you can modify the provided PHP code to display only those news articles which were published within the last 12 hours using the Simple HTML DOM Parser library. Here's how you can do it:
```
// Load the HTML file using Simple HTML DOM Parser
$html = file_get_html('https://example.com/news');
// Loop through all the news articles
foreach($html->find('div.news-article') as $article){
 // Get the headline, summary, and publication date
 $headline = $article->find('h2', 0)->plaintext;
 $summary = $article->find('p', 0)->plaintext;
 $date = $article->find('span.date', 0)->plaintext;
 // Convert the publication date to a Unix timestamp
 $date = strtotime($date);
 // Calculate the time limit (12 hours ago)
 $time_limit = strtotime('-12 hours');
 // Check if the article was published within the last 12 hours
 if($date > $time_limit){
 // Display the article
 $link = $article->find('a', 0)->href;
 echo "- [$headline]($link)  
$summary
";
 }
}
```
In this modified code, we first load the HTML file using Simple HTML DOM Parser and then loop through all the news articles using the `find()` method. For each article, we get the headline, summary, and publication date using the `find()` method again. 
We then convert the publication date to a Unix timestamp using the `strtotime()` function and calculate the time limit (12 hours ago) using the same function. We then check if the article was published within the last 12 hours by comparing its timestamp with the time limit.
If the article was published within the last 12 hours, we display it by getting its link using the `find()` method and then echoing the headline, summary, and link in an HTML list item.

