Sure, here's a Java program that identifies hyperboles in a given text and provides two other examples:
```java
import java.util.regex.Matcher;
import java.util.regex.Pattern;
public class HyperboleIdentifier {

 public static void main(String[] args) {
 String text = "I'm so hungry I could eat a horse. My backpack weighs a ton. This book weighs a ton too.";
 String hyperbolePattern = "(?i)bsob.*?b(hungry|thirsty|tired|excited|happy|angry|sad)b.*?b(could|can)b.*?b(eat|drink|sleep)b.*?b(as)?(horse|elephant|whale|mountain)b.*?";
 Pattern pattern = Pattern.compile(hyperbolePattern);
 Matcher matcher = pattern.matcher(text);
 while (matcher.find()) {
 System.out.println("Hyperbole found: " + matcher.group());
 }
 }
}
```
This program uses regular expressions to match hyperboles in a given text. The regular expression pattern used in this program is `(?i)bsob.*?b(hungry|thirsty|tired|excited|happy|angry|sad)b.*?b(could|can)b.*?b(eat|drink|sleep)b.*?b(as)?(horse|elephant|whale|mountain)b.*?`, which matches any sentence that contains the word "so" followed by an adjective (such as "hungry" or "tired"), the words "could" or "can", a verb (such as "eat" or "drink"), and an exaggerated noun (such as "horse" or "elephant").
Using this program, we can identify the following hyperboles in the given text:
- "I'm so hungry I could eat a horse"
- "My backpack weighs a ton"
- "This book weighs a ton too"
Two other examples of hyperboles are:
- "I'm so tired I could sleep for a week"
- "This suitcase is so heavy I think it weighs a ton"
The context in which each hyperbole is used contributes to the overall meaning of the statement. In the first example, the speaker is exaggerating their hunger to emphasize how much they want to eat. In the second example, the speaker is exaggerating the weight of their backpack to emphasize how heavy it feels. In the third example, the speaker is using the hyperbole to compare the weight of the book to something that is extremely heavy.
In the fourth example, the speaker is exaggerating their tiredness to emphasize how exhausted they feel. In the fifth example, the speaker is exaggerating the weight of the suitcase to emphasize how difficult it is to carry.

