Relational databases store data in tables with predefined columns and rows, and use SQL (Structured Query Language) to query and manipulate the data. Non-relational databases, on the other hand, use a variety of data structures such as key-value pairs, document-based models, or graph-based models, and use query languages specific to their data structure.
For example, consider a simple database that stores information about books. In a relational database, the data might be organized in two tables: one for authors and one for books. The tables would be linked by a foreign key that references the author's ID in the author table. A query to retrieve all books by a particular author would involve joining the two tables and filtering by the author's name:
```
SELECT books.title FROM books
JOIN authors ON books.author_id = authors.id
WHERE authors.name = 'J.K. Rowling';
```
In a non-relational database like MongoDB, the data might be organized as a collection of documents, each representing a book and containing information about the title, author, and other attributes. A query to retrieve all books by a particular author would involve filtering by the author's name within the book documents:
```
db.books.find({ author: 'J.K. Rowling' });
```
Here's an example C# code snippet that demonstrates how to retrieve data from each type of database using their respective query languages:
For a relational database using SQL:
```csharp
using System.Data.SqlClient;
string connectionString = "Data Source=myServerAddress;Initial Catalog=myDataBase;User Id=myUsername;Password=myPassword;";
string queryString = "SELECT books.title FROM books JOIN authors ON books.author_id = authors.id WHERE authors.name = 'J.K. Rowling';";
using (SqlConnection connection = new SqlConnection(connectionString))
{
 SqlCommand command = new SqlCommand(queryString, connection);
 connection.Open();
 SqlDataReader reader = command.ExecuteReader();
 while (reader.Read())
 {
 Console.WriteLine(reader["title"].ToString());
 }
 reader.Close();
}
```
For a non-relational database using MongoDB:
```csharp
using MongoDB.Driver;
string connectionString = "mongodb://localhost:27017";
string databaseName = "myDatabase";
string collectionName = "books";
string authorName = "J.K. Rowling";
var client = new MongoClient(connectionString);
var database = client.GetDatabase(databaseName);
var collection = database.GetCollection(collectionName);
var filter = Builders.Filter.Eq("author", authorName);
var results = collection.Find(filter);
foreach (var result in results.ToEnumerable())
{
 Console.WriteLine(result["title"].ToString());
}
```

