```cpp
#include 
#include 
using namespace std;
bool isPrime(int n) {
 if (n <= 1) {
 return false;
 }
 for (int i = 2; i <= n / 2; ++i) {
 if (n % i == 0) {
 return false;
 }
 }
 return true;
}
int main() {
 int n;
 cout << "Enter the upper limit: ";
 cin >> n;
 vector primes;
 for (int i = 2; i <= n; ++i) {
 if (isPrime(i) && i % 15 == 0) {
 primes.push_back(i);
 }
 }
 cout << "Prime numbers that are also divisible by 15: ";
 for (int prime : primes) {
 cout << prime << " ";
 }
 cout << endl;
 return 0;
}
```
The `isPrime` function checks whether a number is prime or not. The `main` function prompts the user to enter an upper limit, generates all prime numbers that are also divisible by 15 up to the upper limit, and prints them out. The program uses a vector to store the prime numbers that are also divisible by 15.

Here's the MATLAB code to calculate the ratio of the mass of Earth to Mars using the symbolic math toolbox:
```
syms m_Earth m_Mars
